rs.layer.LayerParameters\u0096\u00B0c\u00E0\u00DEqSp\u0002\u0000\u0011Z\u0000\u0012customMapOwnerFlagI\u0000\ftempManualIdL\u0000\fadvancedOnlyt\u0000\u0013Ljava/lang/Boolean;L\u0000\u0008categoryq\u0000~\u0000\u0002L\u0000\u0008citationq\u0000~\u0000\u0002L\u0000\u000Bdescriptionq\u0000~\u0000\u0002L\u0000\u0002idq\u0000~\u0000\u0002L\u0000\nlayergroupq\u0000~\u0000\u0002L\u0000\u0005linksq\u0000~\u0000\u0002L\u0000\nlp_versiont\u0000\u0012Ljava/lang/Double;L\u0000\u0004nameq\u0000~\u0000\u0002L\u0000\u0007optionst\u0000\u0015Ljava/util/ArrayList;L\u0000\u0007serversq\u0000~\u0000\u001FL\u0000\u000Bsubcategoryq\u0000~\u0000\u0002L\u0000\u0005topicq\u0000~\u0000\u0002L\u0000\u0004typeq\u0000~\u0000\u0002L\u0000\u0005unitsq\u0000~\u0000\u0002xp\u0000\u0000\u0000'\u0010sr\u0000\u0011java.lang.Boolean\u00CD r\u0080\u00D5\u009C\u00FA\u00EE\u0002\u0000\u0001Z\u0000\u0005valuexp\u0000t\u0000\u0007Imageryt\u0005\u00FDZoom\: 1-32\nHill, J. R., and P. R. Christensen (2017),Well-preserved low thermal inertia ejecta deposits surrounding young secondary impact craters on Mars,J. Geophys. Res. Planets, 122, 1276\u00C2\u00961299,doi\:10.1002/2016JE005210.\nEdwards, C. S., K. J. Nowicki, P. R. Christensen, J. Hill, N. Gorelick, and K. Murray (2011), Mosaicking of global planetary image datasets\: 1. Techniques and data processing for Thermal Emission Imaging System (THEMIS) multi-spectral data, J. Geophys. Res., 116, E10008, doi\:10.1029/2010JE003755.\nSmith, D. E., et al. (2001), Mars Orbiter Laser Altimeter\: Experiment summary after the first year of global mapping of Mars, J. Geophys. Res., 106, 23,689\u00C2\u009623,722, doi\:10.1029/2000JE001364.\n\nZoom\: 64-512\nHill, J. R., and P. R. Christensen (2017),Well-preserved low thermal inertia ejecta deposits surrounding young secondary impact craters on Mars,J. Geophys. Res. Planets, 122, 1276\u00C2\u00961299,doi\:10.1002/2016JE005210.\nEdwards, C. S., K. J. Nowicki, P. R. Christensen, J. Hill, N. Gorelick, and K. Murray (2011), Mosaicking of global planetary image datasets\: 1. Techniques and data processing for Thermal Emission Imaging System (THEMIS) multi-spectral data, J. Geophys. Res., 116, E10008, doi\:10.1029/2010JE003755.\n\nZoom\: 1024-16384\nA Global, Blended CTX Mosaic of Mars with Vectorized Seam Mapping\: A New Mosaicking Pipeline Using Principles of Non-Destructive Image Editing. J. L. Dickson, L. A. Kerber, C. I. Fassett and B. L. Ehlmann, 49th Lunar and Planetary Science Conference 2018 (LPI Contrib. No. 2083).t\u000E\u0010Zoom\: 1-32\nTHEMIS Day IR with Colorized MOLA Elevation 100 meter/pixel Global Mosaic version 13.4.\nThis product was created by the staff of the Mars Space Flight Facility at Arizona State University, Tempe, Arizona, USA. The daytime infrared relative temperature mosaic basemap is composed of 26,204 images acquired during the first ~15 years of the Mars Odyssey mission. The MOLA global elevation map was acquired during the first ~4 years of the Mars Global Surveyor mission. The MOLA elevation data was colorized using the standard MOLA color scheme and linear binning between -8,000m and +14,000m. Each of the resulting RGB bands was then multiplied by a shaded relief map generated from the MOLA elevation data and illuminated from the west, roughly replicating the illumination conditions at the local time observed by THEMIS. The resulting colorized shaded relief map was then converted into HSV space, the value was increased by 50% and then converted back to RGB space, in order to make the colorization more visibly appealing. The RGB bands of the colorized shaded relief map were then multiplied by the THEMIS Day IR global mosaic. The resulting map was then converted into HSV space, the saturation was decreased by 20%, the value was increased by 75%, and then converted back into RGB space. These color adjustments were performed to make the final product more visually appealing. This product uses the THEMIS Day IR 100m Global Mosaic (v13.4) as the basemap. This product was produced by J.R. Hill and finalized on May 18, 2020.\n\nZoom\: 64-512\nTHEMIS Day IR 100 meter/pixel Global Mosaic version 13.4. This product was created by the staff of the Mars Space Flight Facility at Arizona State University, Tempe, Arizona, USA. This daytime infrared relative temperature mosaic is composed of 26,204 images acquired during the first ~15 years of the Mars Odyssey mission. This product was produced by J.R. Hill and finalized on May 8, 2020.\n\nZoom\: 1024-16384\nThe Bruce Murray Laboratory for Planetary Visualization at the California Institute of Technology has generated a seam-corrected and seam-mapped mosaic of Mars rendered at 5.0 m/px using Context Camera (CTX) data from the Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter (MRO). The MRO/CTX instrument was built and operated by Malin Space Science Systems and the Jet Propulsion Laboratory. The CTX Mosaic project has been lead by Jay Dickson (Caltech).\n\nSignificant help has been provided by Bethany Ehlmann (Caltech/JPL), Laura Kerber (JPL), Caleb Fassett (MSFC), Daven Quinn (U. Wisconsin), and Trent Hare (USGS). We are thankful for the effort of Lucian Plesea of ESRI to make the beta01 release streamable. This project has benefited from feedback we have received from the scientists listed below who have scrutinized regional samples for us that helped produce this beta release.\n\nJenny Whitten (Tulane), Colin Dundas (USGS), Christopher Edwards (NAU), Marisa Palucis (Dartmouth), Lulu Pan (Univ. Lyon), Debra Needham (MSFC), Ellen Leask (Caltech), Erica Jawin (Smithsonian), Mark Salvatore (NAU), Jim Head (Brown), Vivian Sun (JPL), Mike\nBramble (Brown), Brad Thomson (UTK), Nathan Williams (JPL), Keenan Golder (UTK), Jack Mustard (Brown), Adeene Denton (Brown), Abby Fraeman (JPL), Ingrid Daubar (JPL), Chris Kremer (Brown), Serina Diniega (JPL), Becky Williams (PSI), Ashley Palumbo (Brown),\nKaitlyn Stacey (UT Dallas), Tim Goudge (UT Austin), Edwin Kite (U. Chicago), Jacob Widmer (UMD), Devon Burr (UTK), Claire Mondro (UTK), Chris Yen (Brown), Jim Skinner (USGS), Susan Conway (U. Nantes), and the JMARS Team (ASU).\nASU ingest date\: 5/8/19t\u0000\u00044237t\u0000\u0003allt\u0001-,https\://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/2016JE005210,https\://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1029/2000JE001364,https\://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/2016JE005210,http\://murray-lab.caltech.edu/CTX/,https\://www.hou.usra.edu/meetings/lpsc2018/pdf/2480.pdf,sr\u0000\u0010java.lang.Double\u0080\u00B3\u00C2J)k\u00FB\u0004\u0002\u0000\u0001D\u0000\u0005valuexr\u0000\u0010java.lang.Number\u0086\u00AC\u0095\u001D\u000B\u0094\u00E0\u008B\u0002\u0000\u0000xp?\u00F0\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000t\u0000\fExplore Marssr\u0000\u0013java.util.ArrayListx\u0081\u00D2\u001D\u0099\u00C7a\u009D\u0003\u0000\u0001I\u0000\u0004sizexp\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0002w\u0004\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0002t\u0000\u000Emars_auto_zoomt\u0000\nsigma\:1024xsq\u0000~\u0000-\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0001w\u0004\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0001t\u0000\u0013ms-ctx.mars.asu.eduxt\u0000\u0000t\u0000\u000EGlobal Mosaicst\u0000\u0003mapq\u0000~\u00003sq\u0000~\u0000\u0006sq\u0000~\u0000\u0008sr\u00009edu.asu.jmars.layer.map2.stages.composite.SingleComposite\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0002\u0002\u0000\u0000xq\u0000~\u0000\u000Esr\u0000Aedu.asu.jmars.layer.map2.stages.composite.SingleCompositeSettings\u00D6\u001A\u00AF\u008A\u00A79\u00DC\u00AD\u0002\u0000\u0000xq\u0000~\u0000\u0014xuq\u0000~\u0000\u0018\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0001ur\u0000\![Ledu.asu.jmars.layer.map2.Stage;v\u0008\u00F42\u00A9\u00A3\u0010\u00F0\u0002\u0000\u0000xp\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0001sr\u00001edu.asu.jmars.layer.map2.stages.SigmaStretchStage\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0002\u0002\u0000\u0000xq\u0000~\u0000\u000Fsr\u00009edu.asu.jmars.layer.map2.stages.SigmaStretchStageSettings\u00A3\u000EH\u00C7\u00E4\u00A7\u009C\u009B\u0002\u0000\u0002D\u0000\u0006minPPDD\u0000\u0008variancexq\u0000~\u0000\u0014@\u0090\u0